    {
      "id": "ni68ija",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1. EVOLUTION OF MONEY"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Money supply in India is classified into four measures: M1, M2, M3, and M4, ranging from most liquid to least liquid.",
        "High-Powered Money (M0) includes total currency with the public, vault cash of banks, and bankers' deposits with the Reserve Bank of India.",
        "Fisher's Quantity Theory of Money (MV = PT) asserts a direct relationship between money supply and price levels, assuming constant velocity and transaction volume.",
        "Keynes' Liquidity Preference Theory identifies three primary motives for holding money: transactionary, precautionary, and speculative.",
        "A Liquidity Trap is an economic scenario where interest rates fall so low that speculative demand for money becomes perfectly elastic, rendering monetary policy ineffective."
      ],
      "id": "ygobkdq"
    },
